WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.437
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.657
What computer science
paper most influenced you?

00:00:09.657 --> 00:00:11.240
This came out when
I was a high school

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:15.020
student, but the universal
gripper, which was basically

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.750
just a balloon filled
with coffee grounds,

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:20.300
and it was able to pick up
a wide variety of objects.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.910
And that was a very cool
paper of like, wow, you know,

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.780
the stuff that I'm doing in high
school actually can go beyond,

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.370
and even very simple techniques
can be very powerful.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:31.520
I'd say one of the very
first deep learning papers

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.950
that I read was DeepMind's
paper on building

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:39.040
autonomous agents that can learn
the game of Atari from scratch.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.690
And this was incredibly
influential to me.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:44.870
There was a paper called
"Equality of Opportunity

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.750
and Supervised
Learning" that came out

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.370
the year I started my PhD.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:51.680
And it was about fairness
in machine learning,

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.020
which ended up being a huge
component of my research.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.890
So it's thinking about
how machine learning

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.070
impacts people and maybe impacts
different groups differently.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:02.660
And that has really
changed the way

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:06.710
I think about AI and AI's
responsibility in society.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:09.380
It's actually a book
by Marc Raibert called

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.580
Legged Robots that Balance.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:15.890
A group of papers
from sociologists

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:18.350
and anthropologists that were
talking about disparities

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:19.560
in health care.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.070
And that was-- those papers
were from a long time

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.740
ago, so they weren't really
related to computer science.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.070
But reading them sort of made me
realize how important that work

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:31.220
was to the type of machine
learning applied to health care

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:34.050
that I was working
on at the time.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:38.390
And so there was a paper
that came out back in 2013

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.630
by some people here
and at Disney research.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:44.630
I think the paper is called
"Computational Design

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:47.870
of Mechanical Characters."

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:51.440
And this was a really exciting
paper to me at the time,

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.250
because it was the first one
or one of the first ones that

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.640
went from, OK, you make
a digital character

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.020
and you want it to
do some sort of task,

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:02.060
and then you want to
fabricate it at the end.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.710
How do you do all of
these three things

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:05.620
in a way that's in
some way optimal?

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.620
And this was sort of
coming out at the same time

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.185
that I was applying
for PhD programs.

00:02:09.185 --> 00:02:11.810
So when I saw that, I was like,
OK, the thing that I'm thinking

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:13.880
I want to do, that's not crazy.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:15.758
There's people working
in this direction.

00:02:15.758 --> 00:02:17.300
It's definitely
going to be possible.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.860
Let's make it happen.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

